[Ross has two ways of making plurals 1. to make them end in consonant s, and that works after almost every consonant that he can make it end after, where it will sound and you can here the difference between the s and the consonant, 2.to make them in sibilants iz or es, which will work for churches, glasses, and which ends up producing things like cowses, freezes. The only exceptions are a few well-learned forms like keys, but he doesn't even know cows very well. The last thing that he is learning is that you just need a z after a vowel. He hasn't heard very many of those.]
[In the past tense Ross does very nice on his affix checking. He seems to regularly accepts words that end in a "t" particularly, and a "d" somewhat less, as already being past tense, and doesn't continue to mark them. That extends even to something like "fight" which you you think he might know the past tense of, like "fought" but he doesn't, but he really likes "they fight" as if it were the past tense, and that's the past tense he gives. There are a few past tenses that he doesn't want to say at all, like "ate"]
